/* Body text for the Today's Zaman columns that survive only as text: the
   todayszaman.com edition of each, from Şahin Alpay's own export of his
   columns (2013-2016). The rest of the outlet is page clippings with no
   transcribed body. Loaded on demand through bodyRegistry.ts. */
export const todaysZamanBodies: Record<string, string[]> = {
  'turks-and-kurds-have-common-interests': [
    'Turkey entered the new year with revived hopes of an end to the armed insurgency led by the Kurdistan Workers\' Party (PKK) that has claimed 40,000 lives in the past nearly 30 years. Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdoğan disclosed in the last days of 2012 that talks with Abdullah Öcalan, imprisoned leader of the PKK, to put a definitive end to the armed conflict had started and would be continued if positive signs were received. The first days of 2013 witnessed the first visit to Öcalan of representatives of the Peace and Democracy Party (BDP), the political wing of the PKK. Cautious optimism spread when the main opposition Republican People\'s Party (CHP) leader Kemal Kılıçdaroğlu expressed support for the ongoing talks.',
    'The truth that has increasingly become evident, as Ankara gave up the denial of the Kurds and started to gradually recognize their identity rights, is that Turks and Kurds have common interests. Take the Kurds of Turkey. In the classical Ottoman period both Kurds and Turks belonged to the “Muslim nation.” Together they fought against foreign invasion and partition at the end of World War I, and together founded the Republic of Turkey. Turkey is homeland to the Kurds as much as it is for the Turks. Istanbul is the largest city of both. Most of them both vote for the same Justice and Democracy Party (AKP). These facts basically explain why the PKK has long given up separatism and demands regional autonomy instead. When Turkey recognizes full democratic rights for the Kurds, its “Kurdish question” may indeed become history.',
    'Take the Iraqi Kurds who the British separated from Turkey at the end of World War I, and have recently won broad autonomy in the Kurdistan Regional Government (KRG). Their major leaders, Massoud Barzani and Jalal Talabani, strongly state that they favor the closest possible relationship with Turkey. The KRG is economically already highly integrated with the northern neighbor. Turkey is the door that opens to the West and the democratic world for the KRG. This is what Nechirvan Barzani, prime minister of the region, recently said: “Things have changed in Turkey. It\'s simple. Turkey needs something that it doesn\'t have. We need certain things that we don\'t have … And it doesn\'t have anything to do with politics. It\'s an economic matter. They would like, and we would like likewise, to achieve progress. Because Turkey is a very important country for us … We are attempting to play a role so that there will be a political solution for this [PKK] question.” (Time, Dec. 21, 2012.) When Turkey moves ahead to recognize full identity rights to its Kurds and to integrate even more closely with the KRG, it is highly likely that the Kurds in Syria, Iran and elsewhere will assume an amicable attitude towards it.',
    'Turkey\'s policy until the end of the Cold War was essentially to subject its Kurds to forced assimilation, and cooperate with Damascus, Baghdad and Tehran to keep the Kurds down. Beginning in the early 1990s with President Turgut Özal, this policy was challenged by a new one aiming to recognize Kurdish rights inside and establish close ties with the Kurds outside, which eventually prevailed with the coming to power of the AKP. The denial of the Kurds has come to an end, and significant (though insufficient) measures have been adopted to recognize their identity rights, while the policy of cooperation with the neighboring states against the Kurds expired.',
    'What obliged Ankara to gradually change its Kurdish policy is surely national interests. Two things were better understood by time: 1) Turkey\'s territorial integrity cannot be preserved by denial and force. 2) Turks and Kurds have not only a common history and culture but also common interests. To such an extent that a Western observer is prompted to conclude: “Since 2008, in reversal of earlier policy, the government of Turkish Prime Minister Erdoğan has been pursuing ‘full economic integration\' with Iraqi Kurdistan … Turkey\'s extraordinary courtship with Iraqi Kurds has moved from the merely economic to the political and strategic, too (David Hirst, Guardian, Jan. 4, 2013).',
  ],
  'why-love-hate-with-the-west': [
    'I have long been intrigued by the love-hate relationship with the West widespread among Turkey\'s elites, particularly the Kemalists, but surely not restricted to them.',
    'The West that is admired and emulated is simultaneously perceived as a force opposed to Turkey\'s progress and covertly aiming to subvert it. The Kemalist cliché “Westernization despite the West” perhaps best captures that mentality.',
    'One reflection of the love-hate relationship is a certain feeling of inferiority and even shame before the West, what may be called auto-Orientalism in the Edward Said sense of the concept. Ayşe Zarakol, a young academic from Cambridge University, in a study titled “After Defeat: How the East learned to live with the West” (Cambridge University Press, 2011), analyzes what I call the love-hate relationship with the West. The study compares the reactions of three former empires to defeat by the West: the Ottoman Empire of World War I, Japan of World War II and the Soviet Union of the Cold War. She argues that the love-hate relationship has its roots in the international system, and is thus not specific to Turkey.',
    'Western nations at the center of the international system and modernity have stigmatized the latecomers as lagging behind the West, as not being sufficiently modern, secular and civilized. Those subject to this stigmatization have gradually internalized it and developed feelings of inferiority, guilt and shame.',
    'In the foreword to the Turkish edition of her book, Zarakol discusses the consequences for Turkey: “In the 19th century many non-European countries were stigmatized and vilified for not being European. Worst still, elites (and to a certain extent even the people receiving modern education) in these countries internalized such stigma. Who would deny that we are still trying to cope with this pathology and still have not overcome its effects?”',
    'She points to the difference the Justice and Development Party (AKP) government has made: “AKP leadership sees Turkey\'s relationship with the West differently than former governments and particularly diplomatic corps… This does not, however, mean that it has done away with the problems dealt with in this book, but it has managed to display a divergent, less apologetic approach compared to older cadres… When that new approach combined with changes in the international system and increasing economic power, [as if suddenly] a Turkey with aims for regional leadership and a more assertive stance towards the West has appeared on the scene…”',
    'Zarakol does not, however, neglect to add: “The difference the AKP has made in Turkey\'s foreign policy cannot be denied, but this at least for now is on the surface, since it challenges only Turkey\'s position and not the mentality. Turkey\'s foreign policy is still Western-oriented. … It still sees the regions outside of the West out of this lens, and assumes the attitude of a big brother towards them. … It presumes to know the Middle East well and even to be able to save it. … But the concept of itself as leader of others who are stigmatized is in fact an extension of the psychology of the stigmatization it shares with them. … The difference the AKP has made is also under threat of dissolving as the party increasingly integrates with the state.”',
    'The lesson Zarakol draws for Turkey is this: “All of the above does not mean that we can avoid assuming responsibility for our problems. But the resolution of those problems requires that we do not swing between self-praise and self-vilification. It requires that both the positive and the negative in history are conceived as parts of a whole.”',
    'Zarakol\'s study is a highly significant contribution to the understanding of the psychology of Turkey\'s relationship with the West. I tend to agree with most of her analysis. What I would add to it is that the standards of human rights and democracy have evolved in the West through very costly internal struggles. Only by firmly establishing those standards can Turkey overcome those feelings of backwardness, shame and guilt Zarakol refers to, and consolidate self-respect and self-confidence.',
  ],
  'what-should-turkey-do-with-the-european-union': [
    'Turkish Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdoğan often says things he does not mean.',
    'In that vein, before and during his official visit to the European Union member states in Central Europe, he said that it is time Brussels made up its mind about Turkey\'s membership in the EU, and if it does not want Turkey to join, Ankara will look for other options, like membership in the Shanghai Cooperation Organization (SCO), a mainly security organization that brings together authoritarian regimes in Asia.',
    'The Foreign Ministry in Ankara was quick to clarify: Turkey\'s cooperation with the EU and NATO do not constitute an obstacle for improving ties with the SCO. Ties with the SCO do not represent an alternative to the country\'s membership bid for the EU and its membership in NATO. Soon after, Erdoğan stated that relations with the SCO or the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) had no other meaning than “broadening markets for the country.”',
    'Perhaps the most reasonable interpretation of Erdoğan\'s diatribes against the EU came from Council of Europe Secretary-General Thorbjorn Jagland, who termed them “a warning shot to opponents of Turkey in the EU.” This may be the case, but it is difficult to understand how such a warning can help bring Turkey closer to the EU. Erdoğan\'s claim that Turkey has been waiting at the doors of the EU since 1959 or 1963 does not correspond to the facts. Ankara applied for membership in 1987. And is it not clearly necessary for Ankara to first fulfill the criteria of membership before Erdoğan can cry out loud to the EU about making up its mind about Turkey?',
    'I fully agree with Erdoğan on the other hand, that there are quite a few EU members who fall short of fulfilling the criteria, and pursue foreign policies incompatible with the universal values the EU claims to uphold. It is also true that Greek Cyprus was accepted into the EU in outright violation of the criteria, mainly to overcome Athens\' threat to veto eastern enlargement.',
    'I would also agree with Erdoğan that it is not only Turkey\'s failures which explain only 13 of 35 chapters being opened and only one of those having been closed since the start of accession talks in 2005. If EU support for Turkey\'s accession had continued, the broad popular consensus in Turkey for accession that existed initially might have encouraged Ankara to fulfill the requirements a long time ago. That EU support was denied because many EU member governments, particularly France and Germany, and the majority of citizens in almost all the member states are opposed to Turkish accession. Turkey is regarded as too big, too poor and too Muslim. Turkey may be less poor now, but it is surely (population-wise) bigger, and maybe even more Muslim, although with a constitutionally secular government. I fear that after the end of the Cold War, fear of “Islamic totalitarianism” has replaced the fear of “communist totalitarianism,” at least in a substantial part of Europe, with increasing numbers perceiving Islam to be the “enemy within.”',
    'If the unresolved Cyprus issue is the main reason for stalled progress in accession talks, the main responsibility for failing to solve the problem surely does not lie with Ankara. The EU failed to strongly support the Annan plan and awarded Greek Cyprus, which rejected the plan, with membership. Brussels is about to bail out the bankrupt Greek Cyprus, whose banks launder Russian “black money,” but has no intention of using its leverage to help unify the island. Washington, too, seems to be content with the status quo on the island, perhaps considering that the cooperation between Greek Cyprus and Israel in exploiting energy resources under the Mediterranean will help boost the latter\'s security. (See: Paul Taylor, The New York Times, Feb. 4, 2013)',
    'My position on Turkey and the EU remains unchanged. Accession talks provide the country with a roadmap for liberalizing its democracy and modernizing its economy. Ankara should stick to this roadmap. If Europe changes its mind and Turkey eventually joins the EU, it will surely benefit a great deal. If not, as Erdoğan says, it surely “would not be the end of the world for Turkey.” What is important for Turkey is to consolidate democracy, the rule of law and human rights. Its interests hinge more on this than anything else.',
  ],
  'the-growing-role-of-women-in-turkey': [
    'UN Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon, in his statement on the occasion of International Women\'s Day, emphasized the importance of achieving gender equality in work and politics, adding, “I ask every prime minister I meet, how many female ministers do you have in your government?”',
    'He was clearly reminding us of perhaps the only law-like finding of the social sciences which says that societies are developed in direct proportion to the scale of women\'s participation in economic and political life. The more civilized societies tend to have an equal number of women and men in government. It is surely a pity that there is only one female minister in Turkey\'s current government.',
    'On International Women\'s Day, Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdoğan called on all mothers and women in Turkey to raise their voices in support of the current peace talks with the Kurdistan Workers\' Party (PKK) to end its armed insurgency. He said, “When you [mothers] say stop, God willing, all stops.” He is right. It is surely necessary that women\'s voices are heard and listened to if problems are to be solved. There is no doubt that under the Justice and Development Party (AK Party) government led by Erdoğan, women\'s rights have substantially improved. (See “Sex and power in Turkey,” European Stability Initiative, 2007.) It does not suffice, however, that women have equal rights. It is equally necessary that women have equal say and equal respect with men in both family and society. The conservative mentality that restricts the social role of women to getting married and bearing children, to being a good wife and mother, does not, however, allow for an equal say and respect for women.',
    'The world average for the proportion of women in parliaments is around 18 percent. This proportion has risen to 14 percent in Turkey only in the last general election. The lowest rate of participation in the labor force among women is about 55 percent in the European Union, whereas only 30 percent in Turkey. The proportion of women representatives in Parliament has nearly tripled during the last 10 years under the AK Party government. The schooling rate for girls has gone up to roughly equal that of boys, with 100 percent in primary, 65 percent in secondary and 35 percent in higher education. The rate of economic activity and wage-earning employment among women is also on the rise. It has reached 70 percent among women with higher education. In Turkey today women with higher education are assuming leading roles in many spheres, primarily in business management, academia, medicine, law and the arts.',
    'One of the main reasons for the increasing number of incidents of reported violence against women surely is the resentment among men who feel male domination being increasingly challenged. Prime Minister Erdoğan recently, and rightly, said violence against women is against humanity; it is simple savagery and is despicable. But one of his party\'s provincial chiefs declared in a message on the occasion of Women\'s Day that “it is permissible to lightly beat and threaten women.” This person should be prosecuted for inciting violence against women and immediately expelled from the governing party.',
    'I don\'t believe Prime Minister Erdoğan\'s intention is to shut women up in their homes when he has been insistently calling on every woman to get married and bear at least three children. I believe what he is worried about is that if the fertility rate continues to decline, Turkey is likely to suffer by mid-century from the same kind of demographic problems (population decrease, aging and falling rate of economic activity among the population) as in the West.',
    'Research reveals, however, that in all Muslim-majority countries, including Turkey, the population growth rate is declining, while women\'s average marrying age is increasing and the fertility rate is going down. (See David Ignatius, “A demographic shift in the Muslim world,” The New York Times, Feb. 8) It is clear that these trends are the consequences of ongoing modernization processes, which may mean that Prime Minister Erdoğan\'s calls for at least three children per woman are not likely to be heeded, and that subsidies for families with children may prove more efficient for his purposes.',
  ],
  'a-major-step-towards-turkish-kurdish-peace': [
    'History was written during this year\'s Newroz celebrations in Diyarbakır (Amed), the largest city of Turkey\'s Kurdistan.',
    'A major step was taken towards ending the armed Kurdish insurgency led by the Kurdistan Workers\' Party (PKK), which has cost an estimated 35,000 lives and $400 billion. Turkey\'s Kurds in their hundreds of thousands celebrated Newroz with unprecedented joy in the hope that peace will from now on prevail and that they will no longer find themselves in the crossfire of the armed conflict between security forces and PKK fighters. The vast majority of the rest of Turkey, except for those who thrive on the continued shedding of blood, also rejoiced.',
    'Abdullah Öcalan, the imprisoned leader of the PKK, in a letter read out to the celebrating crowds, ordered PKK fighters to cease fire and withdraw from Turkish territory. He said, “The point has been reached where guns should be silenced and ideas and politics speak out instead.” He was clearly referring to Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdoğan\'s statement at the end of last year. That Erdoğan and Öcalan meet at the same “point” signifies the fact that both sides recognize there can be no military solution to the Kurdish question and that they can both win by talking.',
    'We have arrived at this point because both the Turkish state and the PKK have changed over the course of the conflict. Let\'s think back: Until the end of the Cold War, the politics of a “military solution” prevailed in Ankara. It essentially meant that the existence of Kurds was denied; they were subjected to forced assimilation; those who objected to it were punished severely; and even the Kurds of Iraq were regarded as enemies.',
    'Beginning in 1990, signals for a “civil solution” started to come out of Ankara. The Social Democratic People\'s Party (SHP) published the “Report on the Southeast,” which talked about Kurds and their identity rights. President Turgut Özal lifted the ban on spoken Kurdish, announced that his mother was Kurdish and suggested we debate federalism while he sought dialogue with Öcalan in the Bekaa Valley. Former President Süleyman Demirel declared, “We recognize the Kurdish reality.” Former Prime Minister Tansu Çiller mumbled about the “Basque model.” Former Prime Minister Mesut Yılmaz said, “The way to European Union membership goes through Diyarbakır.” Even former Prime Minister Necmettin Erbakan attempted dialogue with Öcalan. Every time, however, the prime ministers\' will had to give in to the hard-line Kemalist will of the military, and the low-intensity civil war that started in the early 1980s became very dirty in the 1990s.',
    'It was Erdoğan who picked up from where Özal had left off and adopted a “civilian” approach to the problem after taking the military under control. The denial of the Kurds came to an end. Measures against forced assimilation ranging from broadcasting in Kurdish to elective Kurdish courses in public schools were adopted. Changes in the anti-terror law that will soon come into force will lift restrictions on the non-violent expression of Kurdish demands. Close political and economic relations were established with the Kurdistan Regional Government (KRG) in Iraq. And finally peace talks with the PKK were initiated.',
    'The PKK has also changed over time. At its founding in 1978 it aimed at uniting all the Kurds of the Middle East under the flag of a Marxist-Leninist Kurdistan. Confronting the realities on the ground, it first dumped Marxism-Leninism and later pan-Kurdism to begin advocating autonomy for Turkey\'s Kurds. And finally it is looking for ways to replace, if possible, armed insurgency with a legal political struggle for Kurdish rights. His latest statement seems to indicate that he is no longer aiming at a Kurdish nation-state, but searching for a model where people irrespective of ethnic and religious identities will be able to freely live side-by-side.',
    'A major step towards Turkish-Kurdish peace has been taken. For peace to take root it is now necessary that arms on both sides are silenced for good and that a new constitution based not on Kemalism but on a liberal and pluralist democracy which assures the democratic rights of the Kurdish citizens of Turkey is adopted.',
  ],
  'why-is-the-turkish-militarys-political-role-waning': [
    'The growing number of books in foreign languages on contemporary Turkish affairs is most welcome.',
    'The outside world is indeed badly in need of becoming better informed about Turkey today. One of the books worth noting in this context is “The Routledge Handbook of Modern Turkey” (2012) edited by Metin Heper and Sabri Sayarı, two distinguished professors of Turkish politics. The book covers 35 articles by acclaimed experts on topics ranging from Turkey\'s history, culture, politics and society to its demography, environment and economy.',
    'Here I will only be able to say a few words about the entries that I found particularly interesting. Ali Karaosmanoğlu\'s article on “Civil-Military Relations” is highly worth reading since it analyzes the military\'s waning political role in Turkey. Karaosmanoğlu explains this by the following factors: The officer corps has increasingly grasped the fact that democracy, which requires the military to be subjected to the authority of the elected government, is an indispensable part of modernization. They have also increasingly realized that involvement in politics is undermining the professional duties of the army. They feel the growing pressure from civil society for further democratization, while the contemporary international context is also discouraging military intervention in politics.',
    'Another factor which perhaps needs to be considered is the existence of not one but two armies in Turkey: one committed to the preservation of the Kemalist ideology of the state and the other to professional duties, with the latter increasingly gaining ground due to the factors indicated above. The difference between the two trends may be observed in the high military command\'s intolerance of junta formations in violation of discipline in the armed forces and the relatively short-lived periods of military rule since the transition to multi-party politics. In this context, the Justice and Development Party (AK Party) government\'s success in helping to establish discipline in the military ranks should also be considered. The institutionalization of the supremacy of civilian authority is still awaiting, however, the adoption of a constitution that will consolidate liberal democracy.',
    'A piece conspicuous by its inadequacy is David Shankland\'s article “Islam” in Turkey. Shankland is a social anthropologist who has studied Alevis and can certainly claim to be an expert in that field. Alevis, part of whom regard their belief system to be an altogether different religion than Islam, however, comprise only 10-12 percent of Turkey\'s overwhelmingly Muslim population. Shankland\'s views on Islam and politics in Turkey, on the other hand, properly reflect hard-line Kemalists\' position, but are bound to be found highly biased by others. One really wonders why the editors of the book have not been able to find any other author to assign this highly important topic, whose treatment certainly requires broader qualifications than Shankland\'s.',
    'Shankland finds Kemalist authoritarian policies highly successful in secularizing Turkey. Those who are interested to learn about the serious problems that those policies have led to are recommended to read the studied treatment of the issue of secularism in Turkey by Elisabeth Özdalga in the same book.',
    'Finally, a few words about Andrew Mango\'s article on “The Kurds.” Reading the article, one gets the impression that repeated violent Kurdish uprisings in Turkey since the founding of the republic are not due to reactions to the denial of Kurdish identity, forced assimilation of the Kurds and brutal suppression of their objections to it but the consequence of the republic contributing to the rise of Kurdish nationalism by opening free boarding schools for Kurdish students from poor families. This is what he writes: “It was the Turkish Republic that fed, lodged, and trained the Kurdish nationalists who claimed to be victims of official discrimination.” (p. 251) It is much better to read Mango\'s article on “Atatürk and the Kurds” (Middle Eastern Studies, Vol. 35, No. 4, 1999, pp. 1-25) if one wants to understand the Kurdish question in Turkey.',
  ],
  'is-erdogan-mildly-moderately-or-slightly-islamist': [
    'Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdoğan and his Justice and Development Party (AKP) have been in power in Turkey for over 10 years. Considering their performance during these 10 years, one would expect their political identity to have been clarified by now. This, however, is not the case. There are both inside and outside the country those who continue to label them as “Islamist,” although they have long rejected this label and declared to adhere to “conservative democracy.”',
    'There is a broad consensus among Kemalists and Islamists in Turkey that a proper Muslim cannot be anything but Islamist and since Erdoğan is devoutly Muslim, he is by definition an Islamist. Even some who claim to be political scientists adhere to this conception. Abroad, there are understandable reasons why the Israeli lobby in the United States considers Erdoğan a hidden Islamist of the worst kind. It is rather ridiculous, however, that even some of the more sophisticated media outlets in the West continue to refer to Erdoğan as “mildly, moderately, slightly, occasionally” Islamist. This may also be due to confusion caused by some Western analysts who define Islamism in such a broad way that anyone devoutly Muslim becomes an Islamist.',
    'But if Islamism is to be defined in a tangible manner, that is, as a political movement that aims to establish a state based at least partly on Shariah law and advocating for pan–Islamic unity and the elimination of all Western influence, Erdoğan and his party can be anything but Islamist. Erdoğan\'s government never attempted to adopt Shariah law. Far from aiming to eliminate all Western influence, it legislated reforms to fulfill the Copenhagen Criteria to start accession negotiations with the European Union. It never advocated Turkey moving out of the Western alliance and assuming the leadership of the Muslim world.',
    'Erdoğan and many of his party members certainly have roots in the Islamist, National Vision movement, but at least since the founding of the AKP, they represent a mixture of influences from different ideological sources. Let\'s take Erdoğan. He definitely is a conservative politician in the cultural sense, being inspired by Islamic moral and social values. Yes, he sometimes blasts against alcohol consumption, abortion and cesarean deliveries, but has never initiated legislation to ban them. The AKP has in fact enacted laws to improve women\'s rights and women are surely assuming a growing role in Turkey\'s social, economic and political life.',
    'Conservatism is far from defining Erdoğan\'s politics. He can fairly claim to be one of the foremost reformist leaders in the history of the Republic of Turkey. He has implemented reforms towards adopting EU norms, placed the military effectively under civilian control, put an end to the denial of Kurdish identity and improved the cultural and linguistic rights of the Kurds. He is currently leading an initiative towards a negotiated end to the armed insurgency led by the Kurdistan Workers\' Party (PKK) that has cost 40,000 lives during the last 30 years.',
    'In terms of economic policies that have helped considerably to improve living standards and earned him his election victories, Erdoğan can hardly be labeled anything other than liberal. He surely is also a nationalist, but certainly not in the sense of ethnic Turkish nationalism, rather in the sense of religious nationalism or patriotism reflected in his often stated ideal of making Turkey one of the 10 largest economies by the 100th anniversary of the founding of the republic. He may even be regarded as a Kemalist in certain respects, at least in his commitment to the state monopolizing and controlling religious affairs.',
    'It needs to be understood that Erdoğan is not an ideological but a pragmatic politician, intent on pursuing policies he believes will serve the interests of the majority and assure him continued power. The increasingly authoritarian streak in his leadership may be viewed as a consequence of his conviction that the attainment of his ideals would not be possible without concentrating political power in his own hands. Some rightly define him as a “control freak.”',
  ],
  'when-would-the-pkk-cease-to-be-terrorist': [
    'Time magazine in its April 29-May 6, 2013, issue listed Abdullah Öcalan, the imprisoned leader of the Kurdistan Workers\' Party (PKK), among the “100 most influential people in the world.” There is no doubt that Öcalan is highly influential among the Kurds. The celebrations of his 64th birthday in the village in southeast Turkey where he was born on April 4 of this year attracted thousands of people. Those who prayed and felt with their hands the soil around the house where he was born were a clear indication that for many Kurds Öcalan has assumed almost a sacred character beyond his political leadership.',
    'How has Öcalan achieved an influence among the Kurds that goes far beyond the borders of Turkey while being confined for the last 14 years to a prison cell on an island in the Sea of Marmara? There is no doubt that the primary reason for this is the policies of authoritarian uniculturalism pursued by the Kemalist state that denied the Kurds\' existence, subjected them to forced assimilation and brutally suppressed their objections. In this context the torture and extrajudicial killings that Kurdish activists were subjected to during the military regime in Turkey between 1980 and 1983 had a special role to play in extending Öcalan\'s authority. In short, Öcalan owes his influence essentially to Kemalism.',
    'The Kemalist regime in Turkey has contributed to Öcalan\'s influence in other ways than those mentioned above. The view that the “deep state” in Turkey covertly helped the violent PKK to broaden its ranks in order to declare illegitimate the struggle Kurds were waging for their identity rights and justify its suppression by force is not at all unfounded. It is no coincidence that such views are common among Kurdish liberal intellectuals who believe that the Kemalist state has taken advantage of the armed insurgency led by the PKK to kill some of the most valued sons and daughters of the Kurdish people and to destroy and depopulate their land.',
    'It is well known that Öcalan when he set out in the 1970s to unite all Kurds under the flag of a communist Kurdistan through armed revolution was inspired by the Marxist-Leninist movement of Turkey of which he was a member. It is equally well known that Öcalan in order to consolidate his authority among Turkey\'s Kurds ordered not only the killing of activists belonging to rival Kurdish groups but also those who opposed his views within the PKK. This is why even among Kurds who sympathize with the PKK\'s cause there are those who think that the close to 5,000 internal executions within the PKK will have to be accounted for one day.',
    'The PKK has definitely fought for its changing aims mainly through guerilla warfare against Turkish security forces. But it has not abstained from terrorism, from targeting unarmed civilians. It has even massacred children and women from Kurdish tribes that have sided with the state against it and terrorized Kurds who have opposed its methods by threatening and killing them. This is mainly why most Kurds vote not for the political wing of the PKK, the Peace and Democracy Party (BDP), but for the Justice and Development Party (AK Party) in power. This is mainly why a majority of the Kurds is opposed to a kind of autonomy under PKK dominance.',
    'I do believe that Öcalan is no longer what he used to be, that he has left behind most -- if not all -- of his past mistakes and is determined to replace armed insurgency by legitimate, non-violent political struggle. I find it commendable that he has ordered the PKK to cease fire and withdraw its armed militants from Turkey\'s territory. I support his being put under house arrest soon and receiving amnesty when domestic peace in the country is consolidated. For many years I have been advocating that Turkey\'s Constitution meets the common, legitimate and democratic demands of the Kurds. The PKK will not, however, shed the terrorist label as long as it does not lay down arms for good and stop threatening for good Kurds who do not sympathize with it. It is remarkable that Gerry Adams, one of the architects of peace in Northern Ireland, considers none of the issues about Öcalan\'s record raised above in his article for Time magazine.',
  ],
  'turkey-will-not-bow-to-authoritarianism': [
    'Two years ago, in an article for a report titled “What does Turkey think?” by the European Council on Foreign Relations, I responded to the question “Would Turkey veer towards authoritarianism without the EU anchor?”',
    'I explained why the argument put forward by advocates of Kemalist authoritarian secularism that “the alliance between the Justice and Development Party [AKP] and various religious movements is leading Turkey towards elected authoritarianism” did not make sense at all.',
    'Today, even after two years, I do not attach the slightest credibility to the argument that Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdoğan\'s increasingly arbitrary and authoritarian leadership since the last general elections in 2011 has vindicated those who have opposed the AKP government from the outset, while damning those who lent support to reforms that it introduced. Those who make such arguments out of ideological fanaticism close their eyes to the profound changes Turkey has witnessed under the AKP government and do not even try to understand what is happening in the country.',
    'With the AKP government in charge, Turkey has significantly improved the standard of living, expanded the middle classes and become an increasingly open society in all senses of the term. It has effectively put the military under civilian control, broadened the public debate to leave no taboo of the past few decades unturned, brought an end to the denial of the Kurds and even initiated a peace process with the Kurdistan Workers\' Party (PKK). As I have repeatedly argued, the “New Turkey,” largely a consequence of reforms led by the AKP government, is not at all likely to bow to authoritarian rule of any sort. The Taksim Square demonstrations, which have been harshly suppressed by the government, have in the main underlined the fact that the people of Turkey are increasingly conscious of their rights and liberties and that Turkish democracy is more mature now than ever.',
    'It is entirely futile to try to explain away, as Mr. Erdoğan does, the growing opposition from various segments of society against an increasingly arbitrary and authoritarian style of leadership by claiming that they are the conspiracies of “internal and external enemies” who are envious of Turkey\'s rise. Such arguments amount to nothing but self-deception and they blind oneself to social realities. It is true that certain radical leftist groups, remnants of “old Turkey,” have tried to hijack the protests for their violent agenda and that Kemalist fundamentalists who have never embraced democracy want to take advantage of the protests to bring back the military. But that should not divert one\'s attention from the fact that the Taksim protests signify, more than anything else, the existence of new generations in Turkey committed to a liberal democracy. These new generations differ fundamentally from the generations of 30-40 years ago who had not the slightest belief in democracy and were committed to various authoritarian or totalitarian ideologies.',
    'President Abdullah Gül has reminded all that democracy is not simply in elections and that no one should doubt that Turkey will continue to move toward fulfilling all the membership criteria of the European Union. Deputy Prime Minister Bülent Arınç has made the following stark statement: “Our government is committed to the basic human rights, freedoms and lifestyles of each and every citizen. We are open to all demands and criticism put forward in a democratic and legal framework. … We wholeheartedly believe that we can overcome any and all difficulties with mutual respect, tolerance and understanding.” I take these statements by two of the founding fathers of the party that even the AKP will not accept a turn towards arbitrary and authoritarian rule.',
    'For decades since the founding of the republic, the people of Turkey struggled with authoritarian secularism and uni-culturalism imposed by a military that believed it owned the state and the nation. It seems that now a struggle by the people against arbitrary and authoritarian rule by elected government is on the country\'s agenda. In sum, the people of Turkey are learning and maturing liberal democracy through practice.',
  ],
  'turkey-needs-europe-but-europe-does-not-care': [
    'The Taksim Square protests have negatively affected the already not so good relations between Turkey and the European Union.',
    'The European Parliament passed a resolution on June 13 condemning disproportionate use of force against protesters by the police. Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdoğan\'s response was that he did not “recognize” the EP. When German Chancellor Angela Merkel said she was shocked by the treatment of protesters in Taksim, Turkey\'s EU minister and chief negotiator responded by saying that there is a “cost of messing with Turkey.” He said: “Sarkozy, who messed with Turkey in the past, can only go fishing nowadays. … Merkel should not use Turkey as election material." He added: “It is the EU that needs Turkey, not Turkey that needs the EU. If need be, we can say to those countries just mind your own business.”',
    'The Turkish ambassador in Berlin was summoned to the German Foreign Ministry, and in response the German ambassador in Ankara was summoned to the Turkish Foreign Ministry to be told about displeasure with statements by the other side. Visits between Ankara and Brussels were reciprocally suspended. The opening of a new chapter after three years in accession talks was likely to be put on hold, due mainly to Germany\'s opposition (for “technical” reasons) despite being supported by most member states. Meanwhile, the secretary-general of the Council of Europe (CoE) warned Ankara that it has to respect the right of demonstration that is part of the European Charter on Human Rights and the rulings of the European Court of Human Rights (ECtHR) against disproportionate use of force by the police.',
    'What can be said about the new crisis in Turkey\'s relations with Europe? Mainly the following:',
    '1) Turkey is a candidate for membership in accession talks with the EU. The EP has, therefore, the right to adopt resolutions concerning Turkey, whether Prime Minister Erdoğan recognizes it or not. Ankara surely cannot, on the other hand, ignore the warnings of the CoE, of which Turkey is a founding member. It is increasingly becoming clear that Erdoğan\'s campaign discourse in next year\'s elections will be based on a textbook case of populism of a religious-nationalist kind. Involving foreign relations in that discourse is surely incompatible with the dignity and integrity of the Republic of Turkey.',
    '2) There is no doubt that the German chancellor has the right to criticize disproportionate use of force by the police in Turkey when even Claudia Roth, the co–chair of the German Green party, has been affected by that. And Berlin is surely right in finding statements by Egemen Bağış -- referred to above -- in violation of diplomatic politeness. Such statements are surely compatible neither with the interests nor the dignity of the Republic of Turkey.',
    '3) The argument “It is the EU that needs Turkey, not Turkey that needs the EU” is self-delusional as long as members of the EU do not agree with it. Today, most EU governments and majorities of citizens in almost all member states are either opposed to Turkey\'s membership in principle or find it untimely. The EU is going through perhaps the worst crisis in its history, while Turkey surely has quite a way to go before fulfilling the membership criteria.',
    '4) The truth is that it is Turkey that needs Europe and not vice versa. The strongest evidence for this is the fact that Ankara achieved a “Silent Revolution” in its political and economic regime by reforms it adopted between 2001 and 2005 when the EU (particularly the German and French) governments supported Turkey\'s accession and reforms mostly stalled when they later withdrew their support. It may be argued that had unanimous support for its accession continued Turkey could have long-fulfilled the membership criteria.',
    '5) It is quite clear that the EU, by accepting, in blatant violation of its own principles, Greek Cyprus as a member without a solution on the island, has either knowingly or unknowingly lost its “soft power” over Turkey. It is a sad fact for Turkey that the promise of EU accession has effectively ceased to be a factor that inspires reforms. In sum, Turkey needs Europe, but Europe does not care.',
  ],
  'why-is-erdogan-polarizing-turkey': [
    'There were surely those who wanted to hijack the Gezi Park protests to help bring the military back to politics or derail the ongoing peace process to end the armed insurgency led by the Kurdistan Workers\' Party (PKK).',
    'The main message of the protests, however, was that the “new Turkey” which Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdoğan has helped build will no longer allow arbitrariness and authoritarianism in the government. Mr. Erdoğan, instead of acknowledging this and returning to the policy of seeking broad consensus which he pursued prior to the latest general election two years ago, has declared the protests to be the work of conspiracies by “internal and external enemies” to discredit him and weaken Turkey.',
    'Turkey is headed towards election years. Next year in March local and in August presidential elections will be held. In July 2015 parliamentary elections will take place. One of the major consequences of the Gezi Park demonstrations has been Mr. Erdoğan taking an early start to the election campaign with the “respect for national will” rallies that he organized to denounce the protests. With these rallies he also indicated that he is going to pursue an election strategy of polarizing the electorate between those who are with him and those who are against him.',
    'He said that the police, by using disproportionate force against the protesters, had created a “heroic saga.” He humiliated citizens putting forward certain demands about the future of Gezi Park by retorting, “Since when have the feet become the head?” Last week his polarizing discourse found perhaps its starkest expression when he called on his supporters to sue their neighbors who banged pots and pans in protest of the government, adding: “For years we struggled in courts. Now let them struggle.”',
    'There are mainly two theories put forward to explain why Erdoğan has decided to polarize society despite the evident dangers of such. One theory says there is actually no logic behind this strategy. It only shows that after more than 10 arduous years in power, he is showing signs of exhaustion and fatigue. His health may have deteriorated. It perhaps only confirms once more that “power corrupts, and absolute power corrupts absolutely.”',
    'The other theory maintains that there is indeed a logic behind Erdoğan\'s strategy. He is aware that the coming years are likely to pose a number of serious difficulties for him and his government both in the economy and in politics. This is why he is trying to prop up his supporters, oblige citizens to take sides either with or against him, is increasingly resorting to an Islamic populist discourse and is attempting to silence his critics in civil society and the media.',
    'The polarization strategy is yielding results. One of those is the expanding debate in the country on what democracy really means. In this debate Erdoğan and his loyalists advocate a crudely majoritarian view of democracy, defending that democracy essentially means the “ballot box” and that the party that wins in the elections is entitled to govern the way it chooses until the next election, when it will be held accountable for its performance.',
    'Those defending a liberal and pluralist democracy in Turkey have for decades fought against the argument that there can be no democracy without the tutelary role of the military and the judiciary committed to Kemalist secular nationalism, which has led to periodic military interventions that created more problems than they solved. Now, after the curbing of the political role of the military and the judiciary committed to Kemalism under Erdoğan\'s Justice and Development Party (AKP) government during the past decade, the struggle against crude majoritarianism advocating a concentration of power in the elected executive with no checks and balances in the political system seems to be on Turkey\'s agenda.',
    'In Turkey it took over 60 years for a broad consensus on the principle of government by elected politicians to more or less be consolidated. The hope is that a similar consensus on the principle that democracy is not only elections but also full respect for citizens\' rights and freedoms by the elected government will not take equally long to consolidate.',
  ],
  'is-humanitarian-intervention-good-or-bad': [
    'US President Barack Obama has decided to seek congressional approval for “limited, narrow” punitive military action against Syria for the use of banned chemical weapons against its own people, killing nearly 1,500 and wounding many more. If this action does take place, it will be in the general context of the concept of a “humanitarian intervention” against a regime responsible for the deaths of at least 100,000 of its citizens in recent years and for there being nearly 2 million of them currently seeking shelter in neighboring countries. This concept that has evolved in response to the spread of internal conflicts in the wake of the Cold War that have led to gross violations of human rights is, however, highly controversial. The debate relates mainly to the following questions: If states commit genocide, war crimes, crimes against humanity or pursue ethnic cleansing, who is to intervene and how, and whether this intercession will help stop the crimes or simply create more problems than it solves.',
    'Those who adopt the case for humanitarian intervention argue that if a sovereign state commits atrocities, the international community cannot and should not, on moral grounds, stand idly by. While international law forbids any use of force against a sovereign state without UN Security Council approval, NATO bombed Serbia in 1999 to stop the slaughter of ethnic Albanians in Kosovo without Security Council authorization. The International Commission on Intervention and State Sovereignty, convened by the Canadian government in 2001, put forward the concept of the “responsibility to protect.” This concept assigns the international community with that duty if a state is unable or unwilling to protect its population from such gross human rights violations. The UN decided at the 2005 World Summit that it has that responsibility. While, according to most international lawyers, humanitarian intervention is conditional on Security Council authorization, some others argue to the contrary, saying that there has never been any need for such authorization for military action to stop crimes against humanity.',
    'On the other hand, those who are against humanitarian intervention put forward mainly the following arguments: Interventions are driven by geopolitics and national interests rather than moral considerations. They are often a guise for neo-colonialist or American imperialist interests. They may set a dangerous precedent for future violations of the principle of national sovereignty, which has so far proved to be the best principle for protecting human wellbeing. Such interventions may provide short-term solutions, but undermine long-term peace and stability, doing more harm than good.',
    'What, then, is my position on humanitarian intervention? I believe each case has to be considered separately. In some cases, such intervention is justified. Both of NATO\'s interventions in the 1990s -- first in Bosnia and Herzegovina and later in Kosovo -- surely helped peace and stability in the Balkans, although clearly they were not driven solely by humanitarian causes. The NATO intervention in Libya, too, may not have been driven solely by moral considerations, but it did rid the country of a tyrant. The international community should not have remained indifferent to crimes against humanity committed in Rwanda and elsewhere in Africa. However, the interventions in Afghanistan in 2002 and Iraq in 2003 were surely motivated by the interests of the US rather than humanitarian considerations.',
    'What about a possible punitive strike against Syria? The dimensions of the tragedy caused by the dictatorship attempting to suppress a people\'s rebellion are only too evident. In view of the military, financial and other support extended to the dictatorship by Russia, Iran and Lebanese Hezbollah, I have written in favor of humanitarian and military support to the opposition to achieve a balance between the sides conducive to a negotiated settlement of the conflict. I would positively view the considered intervention by the US and allies in the hope that it may open the way for a political solution by weakening the tyrant.',
  ],
  'where-erdogan-falls-short-of-putin': [
    'I was one of the first commentators to draw attention to the similarities between Recep Tayyip Erdoğan -- then the prime minister and currently the president of Turkey -- and Russian President Vladimir Putin, in a column titled “Does Erdoğan want to be like Putin?” published in the Zaman daily on Feb. 8, 2011, some time before the last general elections in Turkey in June of the same year. The similarities have become more visible since then.',
    'On Oct. 29 the Guardian published a highly noteworthy article by Natalie Nougayrede comparing the two leaders, titled “The two angry men on Europe\'s borders: loud, proud and impossible to ignore.” The article noted the following commonalities: Both Putin and Erdoğan "aspire to be the fathers of the nation and mix nationalism with anti-liberal traditionalism. … They concentrate power, repress opposition, restrict media freedom, control the internet and have cowed the judiciary. … [Erdoğan] sees himself as the defender of Sunni Muslims in the Middle East, whereas Putin uses the Orthodox church to boost patriotism and strengthen Russian influence in the Slavic world. … Their historical narrative is about victimization by the West. … Both claim they were deceived by the West."',
    'The article noted that both Erdoğan and Putin are obsessed with conspiracy theories, underlining: "All political opposition is ascribed to western-led plots. … Channeling public opinion against the West is politically convenient for both.',
    '"Yet for all the similarities there are fundamental differences, Erdoğan and Putin disagree over Syria: regime change in Damascus, as promoted by Erdoğan, is anathema to Putin. … Unlike Turkey, Russia has nuclear weapons and gas. NATO is ultimately Turkey\'s protection against insecurity in the region while Putin sees it as a threat. … Unlike Putin, Erdoğan does not attempt to threaten western countries. He just complains about them."',
    'I agree with most of Nougayrede\'s points, but there are some I can add. Syria is not the only issue Erdoğan and Putin disagree over. Erdoğan is highly upset over the annexation of Crimea by Russia, but has to keep quiet because Turkey imports 60 percent of its gas from the latter and 20 percent of its gas from the other patron of Syrian President Bashar al-Assad, Iran. Erdoğan is getting the Russians to build a nuclear power plant on the shores of the Mediterranean that will be owned by Russia, increasing Turkey\'s energy dependence on Russia while the risks will be shouldered by Turkey.',
    'In the Erdoğan government there is at least one minister who continues to say the country\'s economy cannot grow without consolidation of the rule of law. There are no such voices in Putin\'s government. There is no substantial difference between Erdoğan and Putin in terms of the grave allegations of corruption and nepotism that have been raised against them.',
    'Putin has complete control over the armed forces, sharing basically the same nationalist ideology with the military. Erdoğan has no such advantage. Turkey\'s military is committed to Kemalism, or secular Turkish nationalism, while Erdoğan\'s ideology can best be defined as Sunni Muslim religious nationalism. Erdoğan seems to have achieved control over the military, but there were coup attempts in the early stages of Erdoğan\'s rule, and the future possibility of such attempts cannot be entirely ruled out even today.',
    'The Soviet Union was a military superpower, and Russia has taken over the Soviet army. Russia does not only have nuclear weapons but has modernized its conventional forces. Using its military power, Putin has been able to subdue Chechnya, Georgia and Ukraine. The Turkish military\'s preparedness and appetite for beyond-the-border engagements are highly questionable. This is why Erdoğan government has to invent, rather than outside enemies, internal ones, and in order not to lose the support of his Kurdish supporters he has to engage in talks toward a resolution of the Kurdish problem.',
    'Putin avails himself of a full-fledged Russian-style presidentialism, whereas Erdoğan pretends he has at his disposal the style of presidentialism of his dreams but in fact does not. Turkey has a far longer history in multi-party politics than Russia, and civil society in Turkey is far stronger than it is in the latter. Finally, it can also be said that Putin\'s economic worries due to the fall in oil prices and Western sanctions may be far greater than the ones faced by Erdoğan currently.',
  ],
  'no-secularism-or-democracy-without-religious-freedom': [
    'On a recent flight I engaged in a conversation with a gentleman sitting next to me. First we agreed and complained that, setting aside the periods of direct military rule, the Justice and Development Party (AKP) government had proved to be the most authoritarian and corrupt of them all. Later the gentleman gently said: “However, dear Mr. Alpay, it was clear from the beginning that the AKP had a hidden agenda. But pundits like yourself conveyed a highly positive picture of the AKP government both at home and abroad. You have a responsibility in the situation we find ourselves today.”',
    'He had a point to a certain extent. I personally refused to call the AKP the AK Party (as ak means “clean” in Turkish), arguing that the party had to first prove it was clean and free of corruption. I never voted for the AKP in a general election. I criticized the media, energy and environmental policies of the AKP government and the inconsistencies in its policy towards the Kurds. But I did generally lend support to the AKP government in its first two terms in power, because the economy was improving, the political role of the military was being curbed, official denial of even the existence of Kurds had come to an end, there were efforts toward meeting the demands of religious minorities, restrictions on religious rights seemed to be gradually lifting, and I highly approved of the “zero problems with neighbors” foreign policy. The AKP government in its third term in power has, however, entirely reversed its policies, and is engaged in setting up the most authoritarian civilian rule this country has ever seen. Out of the same liberal democratic principles that caused me to support it in its first two terms of power, I am, since early 2011, increasingly critical of the direction the AKP is leading the country in.',
    'Public commentators like myself may indeed have a certain responsibility in the situation we are faced with today. But it is the kind of democracy that prevailed throughout the latter half of the past century, where the reins of power were in the hands of a civilian and military bureaucracy committed to Kemalism, Turkey\'s brand of authoritarian secular nationalism, which is the main reason behind the coming to power of the post-Islamist AKP in 2002 and its expanding share of the vote ever since. It is the continued military interventions in politics, the official denial of even the existence of Kurds, restrictions on religious rights, and mismanagement of the economy by the center right and left politicians who have never taken a stand against the military\'s political role that is the main explanation for the electoral victories of the AKP. It is the widespread fears that there will be a return to a full-fledged Kemalist authoritarianism once it loses power that keeps the AKP winning.',
    'Whatever the reasons for the dominance of the AKP in Turkish politics today, the fact remains that the country is confronted with the most authoritarian civilian government ever. What is more worrying are the recent signs that the AKP government, faced with grave corruption allegations, is now trying to build a political alliance with the Kemalist military to fortify its power. There are reports that the National Security Council (MGK), which brings together civilian and military leaders, has agreed in its first meeting chaired by President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan that the witch hunt against the faith-based Hizmet movement inspired by religious scholar Fethullah Gülen is to be expanded to include all Muslim religious groups with different convictions, declaring them to be “illegal structures with a legal appearance,” a concept which surely has no place in the rule of law. The pro-government media has reported that the National Police Department, in preparation for a purge in the ranks, has profiled the religious identities of 180,000 police officers and established that 41,000 of them are affiliated with Hizmet.',
    'A country where citizens, in clear violation of its constitution, are profiled, discriminated against, subjected to a witch hunt, and collectively punished due to their religious beliefs can be considered neither secular nor democratic. This is where Kemalist Turkey finds itself in its 91st year of being founded.',
  ],
  'political-crisis-in-turkey': [
    'In the first decade of the 21st century, Turkey had high hopes for the future.',
    'Its sui generis Islamist movement had evolved to assume liberal positions in both domestic and foreign policy. The Justice and Development Party (AKP), founded by the “renewalist” faction of the Welfare Party (RP) led by Recep Tayyip Erdoğan had adopted accession to the European Union as the centerpiece of its platform and advocated consolidation of the rule of law.',
    'With that platform the AKP won three consecutive elections in 2002, 2007 and 2011. Thanks to liberal political and economic reforms aimed at accession to the EU, the country was rendered more free and affluent than ever. Its “Zero problems with neighbors” (ZPN) foreign policy had served the country well in both economic and security terms. With strong support from a liberal minded intelligentsia and civil society, the AKP government was able to overcome military coup attempts against its rule. It had not only been able to dismantle, step-by-step, the assimilationist policies towards the Kurds and initiate peace talks with the Kurdistan Workers\' Party (PKK), it also had been able to forge close ties with the Kurdistan autonomous region of Iraq, long regarded en enemy by the Kemalist establishment.',
    'The reversal in AKP policies came roughly in its third term of power that began in 2011. Then-Prime Minister Erdoğan, considering that he had enlisted the following of at least half of the electorate and had put the Kemalist military under control, began to concentrate power in his hands, seeking to establish a Putin-like “executive presidency” by changing the Constitution that basically is based on a parliamentary system. When at the end of 2013 public prosecutors put forward the gravest allegations of corruption against ministers and bureaucrats, implicating the Erdoğan family too, the AKP government turned increasingly arbitrary and authoritarian not only by suppressing the graft probe, but also by introducing measures to subject the judiciary to the executive and restricting the media. Almost all the gains of the country, in terms of the rule of law, were reversed. Assuming that the Arab peoples\' uprisings against their autocrats that began in 2011 would lead to Muslim Brotherhood governments in all, Erdoğan abandoned the ZPN in foreign policy in the hope of increasing influence in the region through close relations with Muslim Brotherhood parties.',
    'When nearly 60 percent of the electorate voted in favor of the opposition parties in the recent 2015 general election, putting an end to his dreams of introducing a Putin-like presidency and barring his party from forming the government single-handedly, Erdoğan seems to have decided on a hawkish strategy to take back the initiative. He has derailed the peace talks with the PKK, seemingly to win back the nationalist vote lost to the Nationalist Movement Party (MHP) in the early election he hopes to call for.',
    'Erdoğan and the authoritarian and incompetent AKP government he controls are placing the country before serious risks in both domestic and foreign contexts. The PKK, emboldened by its improved international standing may escalate the armed insurgency it promised to end in return for recognition of autonomy for the Kurds.',
    'Erdoğan had long hoped that Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (ISIL), together with other radical Islamist groups, would help topple the Assad regime in Syria, which he regards the main enemy, and also crush the Syrian Kurds\' aspirations for autonomy. Now that ISIL has begun to attack Turkey, Erdogan has decided to counter and agreed to allow the United States to use a Turkish air base against it. The country is now faced also with the risk of ISIL escalating its terrorist attacks and of being drawn into the Syrian quagmire.',
    'Turkey\'s greater problem is the grave political crisis it is now confronted with. The three opposition parties with widely different agendas cannot unite to form a government that can restore the rule of law and reset a foreign policy that has gone bankrupt. How Turkey will recover from an authoritarian, corrupt and incompetent government that the people are increasingly wary of is a question that needs to be addressed.',
  ],
  'how-the-rule-of-law-has-been-dismantled': [
    'Two years ago this week Turkey woke up to the gravest graft probe in the history of the Republic of Turkey that involved members of government, their family members, top businessmen and public officials.',
    'The evidence appeared to be quite strong, but the allegations were never brought before the court because the Justice and Development Party (AKP) government did everything possible to cover up the probe, arguing that there was no corruption and that it was a plot by Hizmet, the social movement inspired by Islamic scholar Fethullah Gülen, to topple the government. Members of the judiciary and the police who conducted the probe were at once removed from their posts; some were arrested and dismissed from their profession.',
    'The AKP government, in its all-out effort to cover up the probe, has since gone on to adopt administrative and legislative measures that reverse the reforms passed for fulfilling the European Union membership criteria. It has taken advantage of the cover-up efforts to dismantle the rule of law and to suppress the freedom of the press. In today\'s Turkey, a member of the Council of Europe and NATO, and a candidate negotiating membership in the EU, no one is secured a fair trial and access to sound information. The accession process to the EU has become a hoax.',
    'The defendants in the cases against the Sledgehammer and Ergenekon military coup attempts, aimed at toppling the AKP government in its initial years in power, claimed they had done nothing wrong, that the evidence against them was fabricated by prosecutors and police affiliated with Hizmet plotting against the armed forces. The AKP government immediately took advantage of the same claim, arguing that the probe was nothing but a plot to overthrow the elected government. A political alliance between the government and its former pro-military opponents was soon formed, and legislation was passed to establish courts designed to not provide fair trials but to detain and convict government opponents. The prosecutors began filing cases to blame nearly all of the crime and murder committed in the country on the Hizmet movement.',
    'The witch hunt led against Hizmet that violates the principle of individual responsibility, a basic principle of the rule of law, began with the concepts of fighting against “a parallel state” and “illegal structures in legal appearance” that have no place in criminal law, and eventually led to the designation of the Hizmet movement as “an armed terrorist organization.” President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan, who is under a constitutional oath to remain impartial, does not refrain from assuming full responsibility for the witch hunt conducted against Hizmet. He has recently gone as far as claiming that Hizmet has committed the crime of “betraying Tayyip Erdoğan.”',
    'The graft probe that went public two years ago is certainly a milestone in Turkish history and marks the beginning of the end of the rule of law in the country. It is no longer possible to talk about a functioning democracy in Turkey. Can it be expected that corruption allegations will one day be brought before the court? On Jan. 6, the cases against some of the public prosecutors and security officials who conducted the graft probe and are now accused of plotting to overthrow the government will begin. The defendants may put before the court evidence that will shed light on the gravity of the corruption allegations. Leader of the main opposition Republican People\'s Party (CHP) Kemal Kılıçdaroğlu says they will never retreat from bringing before the court the grave allegations of corruption. This cannot, however, be expected to happen before the AKP\'s power comes to an end and the rule of law returns.',
    'The most ironic, incredible and bitter aspect of what the country has witnessed in the last two years is this: While almost the entire world is mobilizing against the violent and atrocious interpretations of Islam represented by the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (ISIL) and the like, Turkey\'s government has declared war on Hizmet, a social movement that represents an understanding of Islam that is the exact opposite of ISIL\'s, one that condemns violence and calls for democracy, secularism as well as freedom of religion for all, and respect for diversity.',
  ],
  'revive-the-peace-process': [
    'President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan, the man practically running the government in Turkey, brought to an end peace talks dubbed the “settlement process” between the government and the Kurdistan Workers\' Party (PKK) leader Abdullah Öcalan, who has been imprisoned since 1999. Soon after the parliamentary election held on Nov. 1, which returned single-handed power to the Justice and Development Party (AKP), he declared that the fight against the PKK by security forces will continue until the group lays down its weapons and its militants are entirely eliminated.',
    'Since the end of a two-and-a-half-year truce last July, close to 600 people, including over 100 civilians, have died in clashes between security forces and PKK militants, particularly those belonging to the Patriotic Democratic Youth Movement (YDGH), who dug trenches and declared “autonomy” in some of the towns and cities of the Kurdish-majority region. The number of those killed and wounded continues to rise. According to Kemal Kılıçdaroğlu, leader of the main opposition People\'s Republican Party, (CHP) nearly 200,000 people have fled from the region. The fear that people of the region are not being treated as equal citizens in Turkey is growing.',
    'The situation in the region has begun to worry Turkey\'s Western allies too. On Dec. 22, a US State Department spokesperson said, “…we hope to see a renewed commitment to the political process by the Turkish government and the PKK to bring about a just and sustainable peace for all Turkish citizens.” Regarding the ongoing violence in the Southeast, he said, “While we understand Turkey needs to take security measures, it should also take all feasible precautions to protect civilians and to act consistently with its legal obligations.”',
    'A spokesperson for the European Commission, in a statement released on the same day, said, “To avoid further escalation of violence, all political leaders need to call for an immediate ceasefire and return urgently to the Kurdish peace process,” adding that “The peace process remains the only way to solve a conflict that continues to claim far too many lives.” He also stated that “The Turkish authorities are faced with difficult challenges due to the threat posed by the PKK and by other terrorist groups at its borders. But it is crucial to keep the response contained and avoid effects on the local population,” and he reiterated the EU\'s “willingness to extend its support in order to reach a positive resolution to the ongoing crisis.”',
    'The deadly clashes in the region have alarmed international human rights and conflict resolution groups. Human Rights Watch (HRW), in a report released on Dec. 22, which pointed to the rising number of civilian casualties, called on the Turkish government to stop the disproportionate use of force by the security forces. The International Crisis Group (ICG), in a report titled “A Sisyphean Task? Resuming Turkey-PKK Peace Talks” dated Dec. 17, underlined what all judicious people are fully aware of: “Direct negotiations with the PKK, in parallel with a genuine reform agenda” that addresses “key Kurdish rights demands, such as enshrining mother-tongue education and decentralisation in a new constitution, remains the only viable way to end the three-decade insurgency. Postponement drives up the death toll on both sides and plays into the hands of the common IS [Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant] enemy.”',
    'Reviving the peace talks may be a difficult task, but it is essential. Turkey can neither achieve stability nor preserve its territorial integrity without making real peace with its Kurds, a peace that meets their demands, is compatible with the requirements of a democratic regime and gains the respect and support of Kurds living beyond its borders. Peace with Kurds at home and amicable relations with those abroad is a strategic imperative for Turkey. If nothing else, do Russia\'s efforts to gain the mentorship of the Kurds in the Middle East, and the requirements of the struggle against the threats posed by ISIL not indicate this imperative? Either a government will take charge that sets aside ideological fixations and realizes this fact, or Turkey as we know it will eventually come to an end. As to the resumption of the peace process, surely none are better placed than Turkey\'s Western allies to help revive it.',
  ],
  'demanding-autonomy-is-not-treason': [
    'In a declaration concluding its two-day meeting in Diyarbakır on Dec. 26 and 27 of last year, the Democratic Society Congress (DTK), one of the organs of the pro-Kurdish political movement, repeated its demand for the decentralization of the state administration in Turkey, allowing for the formation of broad autonomy for regions.',
    'With this, the DTK expressed in concrete form the demand for autonomy it first put forward in July 2011. The declaration stated that the model of autonomous regions it suggested, which seemed to be mostly inspired by Spain, should be debated, negotiated and finally approved by Parliament in order to come into effect.',
    'Prior to the DTK, various pro-Kurdish political parties have put forward the demand for the creation of autonomous regions not only in the Kurdish-majority region but across the entire country. Some pro-Kurdish political parties demand a federal restructuring of the state administration. Last week, a spokesperson for the pro-Kurdish Peoples\' Democratic Party (HDP) asserted that, during peace talks, Justice and Development Party (AKP) government representatives had said that decentralization reforms were being prepared.',
    'The idea of saving Turkey from the current over-centralized administration has not only been raised by pro-Kurdish parties and groups. Those aware of Turkey\'s social, political and historical realities and the requirements of a liberal and pluralist democracy have been arguing at least since the 1990s that the decentralization of the state is indispensable for the consolidation of democracy, the resolution of the Kurdish question and protection of the territorial integrity of the country.',
    'Global experiences also indicate the necessity of such reforms. When confronted with (in some cases violent) demands for recognition by ethnic minorities, nearly all democracies have adopted decentralization reforms in this or another model. Prominent examples are the UK, Spain and Belgium. Even Sri Lanka, where the Tamil armed insurgency for independence was crushed by military force in 2009, amended its constitution in 1987 to grant autonomy to Tamil-majority regions in the north and east of the island. The government that assumed power at the beginning of last year is promising reconciliation measures and to put in force regional autonomy.',
    'In Turkey\'s current circumstances, where the escalation of violence between the security forces and Kurdistan Workers\' Party (PKK) militants is raising fears of growing alienation among Kurds, the DTK declaration can be viewed positively since it emphasizes a resolution of the Kurdish problem through negotiation, democratization and within existing borders. However, the fact that the demand for autonomy has been made in support of the armed resistance to the security forces by pro-PKK militants in towns and cities of the Kurdish-majority region diminishes its prospects of bearing fruit. The demand for autonomy would surely have enlisted much broader support if it was put forward by the HDP in Parliament, in an environment where the PKK refrained from armed resistance.',
    'The number-one issue on Turkey\'s agenda today is the struggle to try to oblige the AKP government to respect basic rights and freedoms, revive the peace process and avoid the institutionalization of one-man rule. There is no doubt that the proclamation of autonomy by armed pro-PKK youth groups digging trenches and building barricades did not in any way help this effort.',
    'Its declarations in support of such groups have brought into question the HDP\'s commitment to political struggle. There is no doubt, however, that the government\'s strategy of squeezing the HDP between the state and the PKK has played a major role in this. Democratic forces that demand that arms are laid down and the peace process is revived should not for a moment forget that the HDP is an indispensable part of the effort towards these aims. If it is largely alienated from the pro-Kurdish movement, the HDP will surely not be able to play any part in it. Let us hope that all who are concerned with the preservation of the territorial integrity of the country are aware of the fact that the cost of excluding the HDP from the political process would be extremely high for the country.',
  ],
  'clash-of-religious-fundamentalists': [
    'Last week Saudi Arabia in one day executed 47 opponents of the regime, including Sheikh Nimr Baqir al-Nimr, one of the foremost representatives of the Shia minority in the country, and 46 others, mostly members of al-Qaeda.',
    'In mass demonstrators staged in retaliation, the Saudi Embassy in Tehran was set on fire. The tension between two religious fundamentalist regimes, one belonging to the Sunni and the other to the Shia sect, in fierce rivalry to extend their influence in the Muslim world hit the top.',
    'There are lessons to be drawn from the current conflict. It is true that both regimes exploit sectarian beliefs to bolster themselves and extend their influence. The conflict is not, however, one between Sunni and Shia believers in general. It is a fight between two regimes and their supporters who subscribe to the fundamentalist interpretations of the teachings of sects within Islam.',
    'It is true that the execution of 47 individuals in a single day is an atrocity seldom seen. The two regimes, however, extensively implement capital punishment, which violates the most basic human right, the right to life. According to Amnesty International, in 2014 the number of countries where capital punishment is legal diminished to include only 22. That year Saudi Arabia executed 102 and Iran 743 individuals. Just three weeks prior to the mass executions in Saudi Arabia, Iran executed three individuals in a single day.',
    'These two religious fundamentalist regimes, which do not respect human rights, engage in torture and execute their opponents -- even gays and those involved in fornication, compete in barbarity. Both are living examples of the threat to humanity posed by religious fundamentalism and bigotry. The president of the Religious Affairs Directorate, the Sunni state institution which monopolizes and controls religion in Turkey, recently blamed secular fundamentalism of crimes against humanity. The truth is that humanity has suffered equally from both religious and secular fundamentalism and continues to do so. The threat to humanity arises from fundamentalist fanaticism, be it of secular or religious origin.',
    'It is misleading to label the regimes in Saudi Arabia and Iran as Islamist. Perhaps the most obvious evidence against it is the fact that Islamists who claim to have formulated a political ideology that rejects both capitalism and communism are violently opposed to both of those regimes. If the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (ISIL) is the pain in the neck of the regime based in Tehran, al-Qaeda is that of the one in Riyadh.',
    'The government of Turkey, which at least constitutionally has a secular regime, should pursue a policy at equal distant to these regimes. It is obvious that Ankara should establish political and economic relations with all countries in line with national interests. It should not, however, maintain intimate ties with these religious fundamentalist regimes, and its leaders should refrain from any statement (like “feeling at home” in their capitals) or behavior to promote their regimes as role models. Turkey should refrain from participating in strategic alliances these regimes form against each other. Ankara\'s decision to take part in the so-called “Muslim alliance against terrorism” led by Riyadh, which excludes all Shia-majority countries, and the conclusion of a strategic alliance agreement with Riyadh are both against Turkey\'s national interests.',
    'Turkey\'s government must adopt a clear stance against religious fundamentalism. It has instead engaged in a witch hunt against the faith-based social movement Hizmet, inspired by Islamic scholar Fethullah Gülen, who preaches an understanding of Islam which rejects violence and fanaticism; supports democracy, human rights and secularism as freedom of religion for all; and emphasizes not the legal but spiritual and social teachings of Islam. Gülen\'s teachings are undoubtedly a most valuable asset of Turkey. That he has been declared the “leader of an armed terrorist group” by the government without any legal basis whatsoever is perhaps the most remarkable indication of the state of madness Turkey finds itself in under Justice and Development Party (AKP) rule.',
  ],
  'the-difference-between-the-pkk-and-daesh': [
    'Turkey\'s Prime Minister Ahmet Davutoğlu, speaking at a dinner at the annual World Economic Forum conference held in Davos, Switzerland, first reiterated the Justice and Development Party (AKP) government\'s discourse: “Our priority is to fight against terrorism of different kinds, be it Daesh [Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant], PKK [Kurdistan Workers\' Party] or DHKP/C [Revolutionary People\'s Liberation Party/Front]. We do not see any difference between these terrorist organizations.” He then went on to say, “We do not observe the same solidarity the international community displays in the fight against Daesh with Turkey\'s fight against the PKK.”',
    'I trust Davutoğlu is among those who know best why the international community, headed by the United States and the European Union, sides with Turkey in its fight against the PKK while urging for a political solution to the problem. He surely is a firsthand witness to the efforts spent by the AKP government for a negotiated settlement until Selahattin Demirtaş, the leader of the pro-Kurdish Peoples\' Democratic Party (HDP) declared his party would not allow for the executive presidential system President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan wants adopted.',
    'We can, however, for the time being leave aside Davutoğlu\'s complaint at Davos about the international community and ask if there is really no difference between the PKK and Daesh. It is true that there is no internationally agreed legal definition of terrorism. Who some call “terrorists” others call “freedom fighters.” Even states are sometimes accused of resorting to terrorism. The term “terrorism” was in fact used for the first time in reference to the government\'s violent suppression of the opposition following the French Revolution. We can still define terrorism as acts of violence that intimidate and kill innocent civilians, and terrorists as those who engage in such violence. With such a commonsensical definition, there is no doubt that both the PKK and Daesh are terrorist organizations.',
    'But there are certain differences between the two. Daesh aims at nothing less than re-establishing the caliphate over all Sunni Muslim societies, and commits the most abominable atrocities. It is not possible to speak to or engage in negotiations with it, in light at least of its behavior so far. The PKK, on the other hand, has changed its aims over time. It set out with the aim of uniting all Kurds of the Middle East under a Marxist-Leninist state. It eventually abandoned Marxism-Leninism, and limited its aims with autonomy for the Kurds within Turkey, declaring it may lay down arms if its demands are met. It engaged in negotiations with Turkey\'s government beginning in 2009, and its imprisoned paramount leader, Abdullah Öcalan, declared in 2013 that the period of armed struggle is over. The clashes between the security forces and the PKK resumed, unfortunately, when the Turkish government, led by President Erdoğan, abandoned the talks last summer.',
    'The resumption of talks, however, seems likely. President Erdoğan had initially said that the talks were frozen. Most recently he said the PKK in the Kandil Mountains or “its extension” the HDP will never be party to the talks but did not exclude talks with Öcalan, who in 2013 said he could support the executive presidentialism Erdoğan wants. While Erdoğan disclosed he is soon to meet with HDP deputy Leyla Zana, former Deputy Prime Minister and Parliament Speaker Bülent Arınç, said Öcalan could be party to the renewed resolution process.',
    'It is not, therefore, at all surprising that the international community, particularly Turkey\'s NATO allies, declare their support for Turkey in its fight against the PKK, but at the same time call for a political solution to the problem. It is obviously in their interest that Turkey avoids civil strife and achieves domestic peace and stability. It is neither surprising that they want Turkey to focus its energies on the fight against Daesh, which they see as the main enemy.',
    'For all these reasons, Prime Minister Davutoğlu\'s complaint about the international community not displaying solidarity with Turkey does not make much sense.',
  ],
  'risks-involved-for-turkey': [
    'Turkish Prime Minister Ahmet Davutoğlu disclosed last Friday his government\'s “roadmap in the fight against terrorism.”',
    'It seems that the Justice and Development Party (AKP) government has, incredibly, adopted the mentality that prevailed in the Ankara political establishment of the 1980s and ‘90s. It is once more trying to view and present the Kurdish problem as primarily one that has to do with security and the economy, as if this approach had not already led to the deaths of 30,000 to 40,000 citizens and proved to be entirely futile.',
    'At the root of the Kurdish problem lies the identity demands of the Kurds of Turkey, whether they are against the Kurdistan Workers\' Party\'s (PKK) violence or not, whether their part of the country is less affluent than the rest or not. The vast majority of them demand the right to receive an education in their mother tongue, a concept of citizenship free from ethnic references and autonomy for their region. If Turkey fails to meet these demands, neither peace in the country nor socio-economic development in the region is possible, as clearly shown by the three-decade-long conflict with the PKK.',
    'This was why the AKP government in its first two terms in power broke with the policies of previous governments controlled by the hard-line nationalist military, introduced steps towards lifting the restrictions on the expression of Kurdish identity, initiated peace talks with the PKK through its imprisoned leader Abdullah Öcalan, and established friendly relations with the Iraqi Kurds. All of these initiatives were steps in the right direction. It is a strategic imperative for Turkey to establish relations of mutual respect with Kurds wherever they find themselves.',
    'The AKP leadership in its third term of power, however, abandoned its agenda towards consolidating democracy and achieving domestic peace, and embraced instead a policy to establish an autocratic, one-man rule by President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan. Talks with the PKK also seem to have been subjected to this aim with the AKP\'s hope of a deal that would enlist the support of the pro-Kurdish movement for an executive presidency in return for the release of Öcalan.',
    'When Peoples\' Democratic Party (HDP) Co-chair Selahattin Demirtaş declared, “We will not let you get elected executive president,” Erdoğan abandoned the peace talks with the PKK and reverted to the policies of a military solution to the Kurdish problem. When clashes between the security forces and PKK militants resumed last July they were no longer restricted to the mountains but had spread to urban settlements of the region, claiming the lives of a growing number of combatants and civilians. Whereas villages were forcibly evacuated in the 1990s, the people are fleeing the violence in the towns today.',
    'A military solution to the Kurdish problem did not yield any results before and it is not at all likely to do so now. This is mainly for three reasons: 1) Kurdish identity has grown stronger than ever. The deadly clashes help the PKK to recruit more and more militants, and it has become questionable whether the PKK is really interested in resuming peace talks. 2) The PKK\'s sphere of influence has spread to include Rojava, the Syrian Kurdistan. It has never before had as many “friends” as it has today. The Democratic Union Party (PYD) of Rojava, which is affiliated with the PKK, is regarded as an ally by the Bashar al-Assad regime, Russia and Iran against both Daesh (the Arabic acronym for the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant) and Turkey, and by the United States against Daesh. 3) In Iraqi Kurdistan, President Massoud Barzani is calling for an independence referendum, demanding the redrawing of borders in the Middle East.',
    'Ankara may still have a chance to revert the balance in its favor by following these three steps: Creating democratic reforms to meet the common demands of Turkey\'s Kurds, resuming peace talks with the PKK with the sincere aim of achieving results, and establishing friendly relations with the Kurds of Syria and Iraq. These steps can refresh the “1,000-year-old brotherhood between the Turks and the Kurds” and help avoid Kurdish aspirations turning into threats against Turkey. Otherwise Turkey risks serious threats to its territorial integrity. Only political ambition can blind one\'s eyes to such dangers.',
  ],
  'turkey-in-erdogans-grip-and-the-eu': [
    '“When [Recep Tayyip Erdoğan] began to demand Bashar al-Assad\'s resignation in November 2011, he based it on the fact that the Syrian president represented an oppressive regime.',
    'Four years later, he has himself become a leader that is trying to seize all power for himself. This is perhaps historical irony, but it is a development that should have been more concretely and clearly recognized by the world at large, particularly the European Union.”',
    'Above are the concluding lines of an article titled “Turkiet i Erdoğan\'s Grepp” (Turkey in Erdoğan\'s grip), which was published in the January issue of the monthly journal of the Swedish Foreign Policy Institute. The piece, which provides an excellent analysis of Turkey\'s adventures under Justice and Development Party (AK Party) rule, was written by Elisabeth Özdalga, a professor of sociology at Bilkent University in Ankara, renowned for her studies of religion and politics in Turkey and the Middle East.',
    'One of the points Özdalga raises in her conclusion is the reaction inside and outside Turkey to the deal concerning the control of the refugee influx into the EU from Turkey, a country which has increasingly drifted away from the EU in terms of democratic values. In this context, she draws attention to the fact that many liberals and democrats in Turkey viewed German Chancellor Angela Merkel\'s visit to Ankara just two weeks prior to the November parliamentary election in Turkey and the postponement of the European Commission\'s annual Progress Report on Turkey until after the election as indirect support of the increasingly arbitrary and authoritarian rule of President Erdoğan, and thus the EU\'s betrayal of democracy in Turkey.',
    'It is true that the EU has remained almost indifferent to the tragedy unfolding in Syria for too long and is, in a way, paying the price with the huge numbers of refugee flows it has been exposed to since last year. The refugee crisis is taking place at a time when the EU has not yet fully overcome its economic problems and when it faces the growth of anti-immigrant and racist movements in most member states, along with the election of authoritarian governments, at least in Poland and Hungary. The EU is in such bad shape that the Greek financial crisis has no longer been a topic in the last few months. It is thus quite understandable that Brussels feels the need to cooperate with Ankara over the control of the movement of refugees. Brussels should not, of course, fail to criticize the growing authoritarianism of the Turkish government. However, it is of benefit to Turkey if, even belatedly, the EU takes the initiative in trying to share Turkey\'s financial burden of having to host over 2 million refugees, introduces visa exemption for Turkish citizens traveling to Europe and revitalizes long-stalled accession talks.',
    'Governments come and go. Erdoğan\'s arbitrary and authoritarian rule is not likely to last forever. In the long run, Turkey\'s interests are more important than the nature of the government in power. If the EU is to share the refugee burden with Turkey and, more importantly, grasp the value of Turkey\'s partnership, the recent deal between Brussels and Ankara is surely a step in the right direction. If Turkey is to overcome authoritarianism and restore the rule of law, it needs to move closer to the EU rather than further away. Strengthening the EU by overcoming the refugee crisis and fortifying its liberal and democratic credentials against the racist and authoritarian trends it faces is surely in Turkey\'s interest, too.',
    'Finally, the fact that Turkey is confronted with the danger of, what one may call, Syrianization, in terms of both one-man rule and domestic turmoil, is not due to Brussels\' choices but Ankara\'s. Besides other policy failures, Ankara has contributed to the growing tragedy in Syria. The fact that the country is currently surrounded by neighbors that are mostly hostile to it should remind even Erdoğan, who once flirted with the idea of joining the “Shanghai Five,” of the value of the EU anchor.',
  ],
  'akp-rule-means-chaos-and-war': [
    'In clashes between security forces and the PKK, and in suicide bombings since last July, the most recent of which took place in Ankara on Wednesday, thousands of soldiers, policemen, civilians and PKK militants have lost their lives and the numbers are growing.',
    'Due to the clashes and curfews imposed in the towns of the predominantly Kurdish Southeast, over 200,000 people have fled their homes. Turkey is drowning in grief just as it was during the terrible years of the 1990s. The condition in which the country finds itself must make everyone question whether the strategy to fight terrorism pursued by the Justice and Development Party (AKP) government unofficially led by President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan is delivering.',
    'According to Erdoğan, the strategy his government adopted last summer is simply “to fight against all the different terrorist organizations at once until there are no terrorists left.” The list of terrorist organizations to be defeated include Daesh (the Arabic acronym for the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant [ISIL]), the Kurdistan Workers\' Party (PKK), the pro-Kurdish Democratic Union Party of Syria (PYD) and the radical left Revolutionary People\'s Liberation Party/Front (DHKP/C), and the fight against them is pursued simultaneously with the policy of assisting the “moderate opposition” against the Assad regime in Syria. This strategy has led to confrontation, alongside Syrian President Bashar al-Assad\'s regime, with Russia, and is in partial collision with NATO allies, with the United States at the top.',
    'It is quite clear by now that this strategy is backfiring and driving the country into chaos and war. Any fighting strategy based on logic and reason necessitates that the main enemy is identified, isolated, and made the focus of the fighting effort. The AKP government\'s strategy is, however, exactly the opposite. Even the Hizmet civil society movement inspired by Turkish Islamic scholar Fethullah Gülen, whose only “sin” is to preach mutual understanding and respect, is declared an “armed terrorist organization” without any evidence whatsoever. The claim that the country is being targeted by “cocktail terrorism,” meaning that two or more terrorist organizations are staging terrorist attacks in cahoots, only serves to obfuscate the real culprits.',
    'If Turkey is to pursue a sound strategy against terrorism, the fight has to focus on Daesh, that aims at nothing less than the domination of the entire Muslim world by the caliphate it claims to represent, and speaks with no other tongue than sheer violence and atrocity. Daesh is defeatable because almost the entire international community, including Turkey\'s NATO allies, is opposed to it.',
    'If the PKK and PYD are fighting against Daesh, it is not logical that they cooperate with it against Turkey. NATO allies differentiate between the PKK and the PYD. In contrast to Daesh, it is possible to negotiate with both the PKK and the PYD, as witnessed by the peace talks that broke down last summer. It is possible to revive the peace process and get the PKK to lay down arms against Turkey through democratic reforms that meet the democratic demands of the Kurds. This is possible because the vast majority of Turkey\'s Kurds, including those who vote for the Peoples\' Democratic Party (HDP), are against violence and in favor of the country\'s territorial integrity. This must be possible because it is clear that pushing the PKK and the PYD towards seeking other allies does not serve Turkey\'s interests. And this must be possible mainly because achieving peace with the Kurds at home and gaining the respect and friendship of the Kurds abroad is a strategic imperative for Turkey, if it is to achieve stability and preserve its territorial integrity. Retired Ambassador Ünal Çeviköz recently expressed this strategic imperative with the following words: “As soon as Turkey is able to seek a solution to the Kurdish problem through dialogue, problems arising from Kurds in neighboring countries will dissolve. This is the strongest trump card Turkey has.”',
    'In the general election held last November, the AKP regained single-handed power by threatening the electorate with instability and chaos unless it held the reins of power. Within less than four months since, it has, however, become crystal clear that rule by the AKP means nothing but chaos and war.',
  ],
  'even-erdogan-needs-the-west': [
    'Once again there is tension in relations between Ankara and Washington.',
    'President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan hollers: “Decide America! Are you with us or with the terrorist Syrian Kurdish Democratic Union Party [PYD] and its armed wing, the People\'s Protection Units [YPG]?...” The two capitals\' accounts of the recent phone call between Erdoğan and US President Barack Obama differ substantially. Ankara says Obama underlined Turkey\'s right to self-defense, hinting at his approval of a possible Turkish ground operation in Syria, whereas there is no mention of self-defense in Washington\'s account. It is reported in the press that Obama neither censured the PYD-YPG nor promised the end of US support for it, and did not give a green light to a Turkish ground operation in Syria. The government, unofficially led by Erdoğan, declared that the recent suicide bombing in Ankara was perpetrated by the PYD-YPG, which the Obama administration declined to confirm.',
    'This is obviously not the first time tensions have built up between Ankara and Washington. This also happened when John F. Kennedy during the Cuban missile crisis in 1962 secretly agreed to withdraw the Jupiter nuclear missiles from Turkey without informing Ankara; when Lyndon B. Johnson sent a letter to Ankara in 1964 saying that the US would not agree to the use of US-supplied military equipment for a Turkish intervention in Cyprus; and when the Turkish Parliament refused in 2003 to agree to the deployment of American troops in Turkey for the invasion of Iraq.',
    'Tensions between allies occur because national interests may diverge due to changing international circumstances: Leaders with different policies attain power, or the same leaders alter existing policies. If 9/11 had not occurred George W. Bush would probably not have decided to invade Iraq. Ankara\'s refusal to cooperate adversely affected relations, but relations normalized when Bush approached Ankara in his second term. Until the “Arab Spring,” Erdoğan, who was then prime minister, was pursuing “zero problems with neighbors,” including his Syria policy, while Obama spoke of having a “model partnership” with Turkey. When the Syrian President Bashar al-Assad regime decided to forcefully suppress the popular uprising that broke out in Syria in 2011, Erdoğan began to seek regime change, while Obama, who began by saying “Assad should step aside,” eventually let Russia and Iran take the initiative in Syria.',
    'According to United Nations sources at least 250,000 people have so far died, 4.5 million have fled the country, and 6.5 million have been internally displaced. Hundreds of thousands of refugees are flooding into Europe and destabilizing the European Union. The 2.5 million or more refugees being hosted by Turkey are placing additional stress on the country\'s stability. Some observers blame the tragedy in Syria on the incoherent and indecisive policies pursued by the Obama administration. Others believe that the Obama administration, from the outset, opted for the resolution of the Syrian crisis in agreement with Russia and Iran, and turned a blind eye to the destruction of Syria in Israel\'s favor.',
    'Neither can the Turkish government\'s responsibility in the Syrian tragedy be underestimated. It expected the US to intervene, and when that did not happen, it began to lend support to the various armed opponents of the Assad regime in a number of ways. It eventually abandoned talks with the Kurdistan Workers\' Party (PKK) and declared the PYD an enemy, pushing it towards an alliance with Russia. And since the downing of the Russian fighter jet last November, Ankara has lost nearly all leverage over Syria.',
    'Where is this crisis of confidence between Ankara and Washington likely to lead? There are voices in the US that are calling for the expulsion of Turkey from the NATO alliance. Washington, however, is not at all likely to sacrifice its alliance with Ankara, although there are signs that it might look for ways to lessen its dependence on Turkey\'s cooperation, particularly on İncirlik Air Base. Ankara\'s dependence, on the other hand, on the Western alliance has significantly grown after the souring of relations with Russia. Even Erdoğan cannot afford to worsen Turkey\'s relations with the US or the EU.',
    'Turkey is faced with the urgent need to reset domestic and foreign policies to secure its internal stability and territorial integrity. Nobody knows, however, how this urgent need is to be addressed.',
  ],
}
